Contract conventions
--------------------
USDT runtime providers carry a PID suffix; profiles select provider*.
Each operation has start/done probes, e.g. attrtool*:...:get-start/get-done.
Pairs execute synchronously in the invoking thread. These boundaries cover
individual API calls or checksum dispatch, not necessarily entire commands.
The manifest records every probe's ordered argument types and source.
All results and errno values are captured before the caller interprets them.
No filename, attribute name/value, ACL, credential, environment value,
checksum, file contents, kernel address or process argument string is exposed.

attrtool delete-start(namespace, nofollow)
         delete-done(namespace, nofollow, result, errno)
         get/set/list-start(namespace, nofollow, requested_size, size_query)
         get/set/list-done(namespace, nofollow, requested_size, size_query,
                           signed_result, errno)
    Each file and link API has its own call site. get/list first query length,
    then request data: those are separate pairs. Positive size-query results
    are required length, not transferred bytes. Short positive transfers are
    reported verbatim with errno=0; the caller can still diagnose truncation.
    Argument/namespace parsing and stdin collection precede these boundaries.

aclread operation-start(acl_type, nofollow)
        operation-done(acl_type, nofollow, success_boolean, errno)
aclwrite operation-start(acl_type, nofollow)
         operation-done(acl_type, nofollow, result, errno)
    Covers acl_get_file/acl_get_link_np and acl_set_file/acl_set_link_np.
    Excludes pathname/stat checks before retrieval, parsing/ACL transformations,
    text rendering, traversal and acl_delete_def_file. No synthetic security
    decision is inferred from a successful ACL read or edit.

catio / teeio / splitio read/write-start(fd, requested_bytes)
                       read/write-done(fd, requested_bytes, signed_result, errno)
wordcountio read-start(fd, requested_bytes)
            read-done(fd, requested_bytes, signed_result, errno)
    Negative result reports errno; zero read means EOF and a positive result
    can be short. These are raw I/O calls only. cat's cooked stdio path,
    split's stdio reads in line mode and wc's metadata-only byte-count shortcut
    do not necessarily fire these pairs. Existing loops and error handling
    are unchanged. tee writes produce separate pairs for each destination.

filesync operation-start(fd); operation-done(fd, result, errno)
    Only files successfully opened reach fsync. A successful return is the
    fsync API result, not a stronger claim about device power-loss behavior.

checksumtool file-start(fd); file-done(fd, length_on_success, result, errno)
    Encloses selected checksum function, covering crc, csum1, csum2 and crc32,
    including sum's default algorithm. Length is zero on failure because the
    helper's output need not be initialized; it does not imply zero bytes
    were processed. Opening files and printing results remain outside.

tailwait / processwait operation-start(change_count, event_capacity)
                      operation-done(change_count, event_capacity, result, errno)
    Covers kevent registration and wait calls. Zero may be a timeout or a
    successful registration with no returned events. A returned event still
    needs caller interpretation. No event payload or signal-handler probe.
    tail's ordinary read/print mode is outside its new follow-mode probes.

modulequery operation-start(module_id); operation-done(module_id, result, errno)
    Covers kldstat metadata calls. kldnext enumeration and output formatting
    remain outside. No module name or kernel address is newly exposed.

envquery operation-start(action, buffer_length)
         operation-done(action, buffer_length, result, errno)
    Covers kenv get/set/unset/dump, including dump-size queries/retries. Results
    have action-specific meanings. Names and values are never copied to probes.

rootchange operation-start(reserved_zero)
           operation-done(reserved_zero, result, errno)
    Covers chroot(".") after directory/setup work, before optional credential
    changes and exec. This pair is not credential-change or exec success.

processquery operation-start(selector_kind)
             operation-done(selector_kind, count_if_available, unavailable)
    Covers procstat_getprocs for live and core-backed handles. The final int
    is a null-result indicator, NOT errno. Count is only read when a non-NULL
    array is returned. Other libprocstat queries and formatting are excluded.

elfbrand read/write-start(fd, requested_bytes)
         read/write-done(fd, requested_bytes, signed_result, errno)
    Only the ELF identification header's read/write calls. A short transfer
    can be a command error despite errno=0. Validation/seeking are excluded.

vmquery operation-start(reserved_zero)
        operation-done(reserved_zero, result, errno)
    Covers vmstat's direct sysctlbyname query helper. Aggregate query latency;
    no individual MIB identity or counter value is exposed. kvm crash-dump
    and libdevstat calls are outside this boundary.

Nine-category mapping
---------------------
Lifecycle/execution: start/completion of the observed operations.
Performance: elapsed operation, checksum and wait latency.
Errors: API errno, short results and unavailable-process-array outcomes.
Resources: requested/returned I/O sizes, process counts, attribute size queries.
I/O: stream reads/writes, checksum input, file synchronization, ELF headers.
Networking: tee writes include its existing Unix-socket destinations; broader
            protocol/network coverage remains in prior kernel/base providers.
Synchronization: tail follow waits, pwait process waits, fsync completion.
Security/access: ACL application/retrieval errors, chroot outcome and attribute
                 namespace/access failures. No authentication claim.
Counters/state: transfer-length histograms, query/event/result counts.
Categories apply collectively where meaningful. There are no invented
allocation, deadlock, authentication, threshold or recovery events.

Build integration and disabled behavior
---------------------------------------
Each affected Makefile conditionally adds its provider and enables the
existing bsd.dtrace.mk generation/link path when MK_DTRACE is not no.
RESCUE and BOOTSTRAPPING explicitly exclude the new providers, keeping host
bootstrap tools independent of target DTrace. These modes were checked at
Makefile source-list selection; full rescue and cross-host bootstrap builds
were not performed. Standard on/off native builds both linked successfully.

Headers guard each USDT firing with its enabled predicate, preserve errno,
and fully parenthesize/cast arguments. Disabled headers do not evaluate probe
arguments. No tracing calls were inserted into signal handlers. Hot stream
operations can produce many events: choose profiles selectively. No enabled
or disabled runtime overhead measurement is claimed; C wrappers may be
inlined, but no machine-code identity claim is made for this round.

Base profiles
-------------
  base-attribute-operations   extended-attribute API calls
  base-acl-operations         ACL get/set calls
  base-stream-io              cat, tee, split and wc raw I/O
  base-file-completion        fsync and checksum dispatch
  base-event-waits            tail and pwait kevent phases
  base-system-queries        kldstat, kenv, procstat and vmstat queries
  base-root-change           chroot API outcome
  base-elf-headers           brandelf header I/O

The existing bsdinstruments Makefile includes these profiles in the base
package under /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles; catalog count is 404.
They aggregate per-operation outcome and elapsed microseconds. Stream/ELF
profiles also histogram successful transfer lengths. processquery's grouping
key is unavailable, not errno. PID/tid/depth pairing is cleared on matched
completion. Start before workload: event loss, process exit or attaching
mid-operation can leave incomplete pairs. Elapsed time includes preemption.
Normal and --pid/--duration generated forms were compiled offline, with exact
selector/argument checks in addition to -Z accepting absent live providers.

Example once installed and the matching provider-bearing tool is running:
  bsdinstruments generate -f /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles/base-stream-io.d
No installation or publication was performed for this validation.

Validation
----------
Native amd64 prepared world sysroot: /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/tmp.
All compilation uses the 16 available CPUs. Maintained validation scripts:
  coverage-fourteenth-base.py         17 binaries x enabled/disabled = 34 links
  coverage-fourteenth-guards.py       34 enabled/disabled guard + errno checks
  coverage-fourteenth-check.py        17 linked DOF inventories and disabled
                                     absence, all 48 contract arities and
                                     firing/enabled offsets, 16 profile compiles
  coverage-fourteenth-smoke.py        100 isolated CLI checks
  coverage-fourteenth-tests.py        134 existing ATF cases passed, 2 skipped
  coverage-fourteenth-integration.py  17 C token-preservation checks;
                                     68 enabled/disabled/rescue/bootstrap
                                     Makefile source-selection checks

The token check removes generated wrappers, reverses call-name substitutions,
and compares remaining C tokens with the saved originals while preserving
string/character literals. It complements builds and behavioral tests; it is
not a runtime performance measurement or compiler proof.

Isolated CLI checks exercise private files, file/link attribute APIs, ACLs,
checksumming algorithms, stream output, fsync, ELF header editing on a private
writable copy, read-only system queries, and waits on test-created children.
kenv and chroot checks relink the actual compiled objects with test-only
--wrap syscall replacements for success and failure. They do not mutate the
real kernel environment or change the actual root directory. These stubs
validate CLI behavior and binding, not real syscall or live DTrace execution.

ATF suites cover cat, tee, split, wc and tail, in both build modes. tee's
sigint_ignored case requires the unavailable porch program and is skipped
once per mode. Source fixtures are linked into the uninstalled test tree;
locale-changing tests retain their own LC_CTYPE. Initial harness failures
(missing fixture paths, missing prerequisite and forced LC_ALL) are recorded
in tests/initial-results.json; the corrected final run is tests/results.json.
No underlying utility behavior failure remains in the executed cases.

